course: religion-ethics
lesson: 101
title: Solomon and the Queen of Sheba: one story in many books, and the legends of the ring and the jinn
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: King Solomon, famous for his wisdom, appears in the Bible, in the Qur'an and in the stories of many peoples. One of the best-loved tales is the visit of the Queen of Sheba, a rich queen from far away who came to test him with hard questions.
explain: The Bible, the Qur'an and the old books of Ethiopia each tell the visit in their own way, and later storytellers added legends of a magic ring and of jinn who obeyed the king. Kipling borrowed all of this for his last Just So Story, where Suleiman-bin-Daoud and his queen Balkis help a boasting little Butterfly.
explain: Historians cannot check every part: the kingdom of Saba in Yemen was real and grew rich from incense, but no record outside these books tells of the visit. We can still learn from the story, as from any great story: about wisdom, about pride, and about helping someone without shaming them.
fact: The Bible (1 Kings 10) says the Queen of Sheba heard of Solomon's fame and came to Jerusalem with camels carrying spices, gold and precious stones, to test him with hard questions.
fact: Solomon answered every one of her questions, and she said that «the half was not told me»: he was even wiser than people had said; they gave each other rich gifts and she went home.
fact: In the Qur'an (the chapter called «The Ant») Solomon understands the speech of birds and ants; a bird called the hoopoe brings him news of a queen of Saba whose people worship the sun, and in the end she turns to God together with Solomon.
fact: Muslim tradition names the queen Bilqis, which Kipling spells Balkis; in Ethiopia's old book «The Glory of the Kings» she is called Makeda, and her son with Solomon, Menelik, becomes the first king of Ethiopia.
fact: Ethiopia's emperors claimed to come from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba right up to the last emperor, Haile Selassie, whose reign ended in 1974.
fact: Saba was a real kingdom in what is now Yemen, rich from trading frankincense and myrrh, sweet-smelling tree resins burned in temples; some people think Sheba included lands across the Red Sea in Ethiopia too.
fact: Later Jewish and Muslim legends say Solomon had a ring, «the Seal of Solomon», with which he could command demons and jinn; in the «Thousand and One Nights» a fisherman finds a jinni shut in a bottle sealed with Solomon's seal.
fact: In Islam the jinn are beings that God made from smokeless fire, mentioned in the Qur'an, and many Muslims believe in them; in folk tales like Aladdin they became «genies» who grant wishes, and an «afrit» is a strong, fierce kind of jinni in those tales.
fact: The Book of Proverbs, which tradition links with Solomon, says «Pride goes before destruction, and a haughty spirit before a fall»; Kipling's Suleiman is ashamed when he tries to show off by feeding all the animals of the world.
fact: In Kipling's tale the Butterfly boasts to his wife that if he stamps his foot Suleiman's palace will vanish, and she tests him by telling him to do it; Balkis helps so that his wife believes him and he is not shamed, he never boasts again, and Suleiman laughs but keeps the secret: wise people can stop a boast without shaming the boaster.
fact: The composer Handel wrote a lively piece called «The Arrival of the Queen of Sheba» in 1749, and it is still often played at weddings.
fact: People still say «I felt like the Queen of Sheba» for feeling rich and splendid, and «a Solomon» for a very wise judge.
exercise: Where is Solomon's story told? | In the Bible, the Qur'an and the stories of many peoples | Only in Kipling's Just So Stories of 1902 | Only in the old myths and books of Ancient Greece | Only in the Thousand and One Nights
exercise: Why did the Queen of Sheba come to Solomon? | To test his wisdom with hard questions | To start a war against his little kingdom | To marry his eldest son | To buy his army's horses
exercise: What did the queen bring on her camels? | Spices, gold and precious stones | Rice, tea and fine silk from far-off China | Swords, shields and armour | Books for the king's library
exercise: What did the queen say after hearing Solomon's answers? | That the half had not been told her | That he knew far less than people had said | That her own king was much wiser | That his questions were too easy
exercise: Which bird brings Solomon news of the queen in the Qur'an? | The hoopoe | The golden eagle | The dove | The crow
exercise: What is the Qur'an chapter with this story called? | «The Ant» | «The Butterfly» | «The Camel» | «The Ring»
exercise: What name does Muslim tradition give the queen? | Bilqis | Makeda | Esther | Cleopatra
exercise: What is the queen called in Ethiopia's «Glory of the Kings»? | Makeda | Bilqis | Balkis | Nefertiti
exercise: Who, says the Ethiopian book, was the son of Solomon and the queen? | Menelik, the first king of Ethiopia | David, the shepherd boy who fought Goliath | Haile Selassie, the last emperor | Aladdin, the boy with the lamp
exercise: Where was the real kingdom of Saba? | In what is now Yemen | In what is now Norway | In what is now China | In what is now Mexico
exercise: What made Saba rich? | Trading frankincense and myrrh | Digging coal and iron in deep mines | Selling furs from the north | Growing rice and tea
exercise: What, say the legends, could Solomon command with his ring? | Demons and jinn | The sea and all its tides | Every army of the world | The Sun and the Moon
exercise: What does Islam teach the jinn are made of? | Smokeless fire | Clay and river water | Air and clouds | Gold and silver
exercise: What does the proverb say goes before a fall? | Pride | Kindness | Hunger | Laughter
exercise: Which composer wrote «The Arrival of the Queen of Sheba»? | Handel | Mozart | Beethoven | Bach
exercise: What do we mean when we call a judge «a Solomon»? | A very wise judge | A very rich judge | A very old and slow judge | A very strict judge
test: «Kipling made up Solomon and Balkis for his book» What is true? | Kipling took them from much older stories in the Bible, the Qur'an and legends | Kipling made up both of them, and nobody had heard of them before 1902 | Solomon is from Kipling but Balkis is from a film | They are from a Greek play written by Homer
test: A friend says historians have proved every part of the queen's visit. What is fairer? | Saba was real, but no record outside these books tells of the visit | Yes, a painting made on the day of the visit was found in Jerusalem | Yes, the queen's diary was found in Yemen last year | No, historians have proved there was never a Saba
test: Why might the queen come with hard questions, not soldiers? | She wanted to see for herself if his wisdom was real | She had no soldiers left after a war | Questions were used as the money of those days, instead of coins | Solomon would only let people in who asked riddles
test: «genies are from Disney lol» What is the longer story? | They come from the jinn of Arabic belief and old tales like the Thousand and One Nights | Disney made them up for Aladdin and nobody had heard of them before | They come from Ancient Egypt, where every pharaoh kept one in a jar | They come from Kipling, who was the first to write about them
test: Ethiopia's emperors said they came from Solomon. What does that show about the story? | It mattered so much that kings built their family history on it | It was only a children's story nobody believed | Ethiopia was part of Israel at the time | Every king in the world said exactly the same thing about Solomon
test: In the Just So Story, Suleiman tries to show off by feeding all the animals in the world, and a single sea-creature eats it all. What is the lesson? | Showing off can turn into shame | Big dinners are always a bad idea | Sea animals are greedy and rude | Kings should never feed animals at all
test: The Butterfly boasts to his wife, and Balkis helps make the boast come true. Why is this kind? | It saves him from shame, and he learns not to boast | It makes him the most powerful and feared animal in the whole world | It punishes his wife for not believing him | It shows that boasting always works in the end
test: Your cousin brags that he can jump over the school. What would a wise friend like Suleiman do? | Smile and not shame him, and talk to him quietly later | Shout at him in front of the whole class | Dare him to jump off the school roof to prove it | Tell everyone he is a liar and stop talking to him
test: «I felt like the Queen of Sheba» says Nan, after getting new shoes. What does she mean? | She felt rich and splendid | She felt like going on a long journey | She felt she had to answer hard questions | She felt tired and old
test: ★★ The queen tested Solomon; the Butterfly's wife tested her husband. What do both tests show? | Testing a claim shows whether it is true | A husband is always wiser than his wife | Kings and butterflies both love riddles | You should never believe anyone at all
test: ★★★ In the Bible the queen gave Solomon 120 talents of gold. If she had given it in 4 equal loads on her camels' trains, and each load was split equally among 6 camels, how many talents would each camel carry? | 5 | 30 | 20 | 24
exam: Identify the principal object of the visit paid by the Queen of Sheba to King Solomon, according to the Bible. | The testing of his reputed wisdom by means of difficult questions | The negotiation of a military alliance against Egypt | The purchase of horses and chariots for her army | The arrangement of a marriage between their children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the historical evidence for the visit? | The kingdom of Saba is attested, but no independent record of the visit has been found | The visit is recorded in numerous Egyptian inscriptions | The visit is proven by the discovery of the queen's tomb in Jerusalem | The kingdom of Saba has been shown never to have existed
exam: Select the response consistent with the teaching of Islam concerning the jinn. | They are beings created by God from smokeless fire | They are spirits of the dead which return to earth | They are inventions of the Thousand and One Nights alone | They are angels who dwell in the heavens
exam: Ascertain the name by which the Queen of Sheba is known in the Ethiopian tradition of «The Glory of the Kings». | Makeda | Bilqis | Balkis | Esther
